<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lyceum (Classical)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">335 BCE</span> · Also known as Lykeion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lyceum was a sanctuary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> Lykeios outside the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> opened his school in 335 BCE. His followers, the Peripatetics, continued teaching there under Theophrastus and later heads, collecting books and research in natural science, politics and philosophy, until the site was damaged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>'s siege of 86 BCE. Its remains were found in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lyceum-classical.jpg" alt="Lyceum (Classical)" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raphael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanzio_01_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
